FADE IN:

EXT. LABURNAM VILLA - NIGHT

Rain sheets across a half-built road. Only two houses are lit. The gate of one bangs in the wind.

INT. LABURNAM VILLA - PARLOUR - NIGHT

A fire, drawn blinds, a chessboard. MR. WHITE (60s, thin grey beard) moves his king into danger. His son HERBERT (20s, quick, amused) studies the board. MRS. WHITE (60s, white-haired) knits by the fire and misses nothing.

MR. WHITE
Hark at the wind.

HERBERT
I'm listening.
(moves a piece)
Check.

MR. WHITE
I should hardly think that he'd come to-night.

HERBERT
Mate.

Mr. White throws up his hands.

MR. WHITE
That's the worst of living so far out. Pathway's a bog, and the road's a torrent.

MRS. WHITE
(soothing)
Never mind, dear. Perhaps you'll win the next one.

He catches a knowing glance between mother and son. The gate bangs. Heavy footsteps.

HERBERT
There he is.

Mr. White hurries out and returns with SERGEANT-MAJOR MORRIS (50s, burly, beady-eyed, face reddened by twenty-one years of India). Whiskey is poured. A copper kettle goes on the fire.

INT. LABURNAM VILLA - PARLOUR - LATER

The third glass. Morris talks, and the family leans in.

MR. WHITE
What was that you started telling me the other day about a monkey's paw or something, Morris?

MORRIS
Nothing. Leastways nothing worth hearing.

MRS. WHITE
Monkey's paw?

He fumbles in his pocket and lays it on the table: small, dried, dark as old leather. Mrs. White draws back. Herbert picks it up.

MORRIS
It had a spell put on it by an old fakir, a very holy man. He wanted to show that fate ruled people's lives, and that those who interfered with it did so to their sorrow. Three separate men could each have three wishes from it.

HERBERT
Well, why don't you have three, sir?

MORRIS
(quietly)
I have.

His blotchy face goes white. Nobody laughs now.

MORRIS (CONT'D)
The first man had his three wishes. I don't know what the first two were, but the third was for death. That's how I got the paw.

He takes it back and, without warning, throws it on the fire. Mr. White snatches it out.

MORRIS (CONT'D)
Better let it burn.

MR. WHITE
If you don't want it, Morris, give it to me.

MORRIS
I won't. If you keep it, don't blame me for what happens.
(beat)
Hold it up in your right hand and wish aloud. But I warn you of the consequences.

DISSOLVE TO:

INT. LABURNAM VILLA - PARLOUR - LATER

Morris is gone. Herbert sits at the piano, grinning.

HERBERT
Wish for two hundred pounds, then. That'll just do it.

Mr. White holds up the paw, embarrassed. Herbert strikes a grand chord.

MR. WHITE
I wish for two hundred pounds.

He cries out and drops it. It lies on the rug.

MR. WHITE (CONT'D)
It moved. As I wished, it twisted in my hand like a snake.

HERBERT
Well, I don't see the money, and I bet I never shall.

Alone later, Herbert watches the dying fire. A face forms in the coals, simian and horrible. He reaches for a glass of water and his hand closes on the paw instead.


INT. LABURNAM VILLA - KITCHEN - MORNING

Winter sun. The paw lies tossed on the sideboard. Herbert grabs his coat.

HERBERT
Well, don't break into the money before I come back.

MRS. WHITE
(laughing)
Go on with you.

She watches him all the way down the road.

INT. MAW AND MEGGINS WORKS - DAY

Noise, steam, belts and wheels. Herbert works near a great flywheel. A belt slips. A shout we can't hear over the machines.

CUT TO:

EXT. LABURNAM VILLA - DAY

A well-dressed STRANGER in a new silk hat paces at the gate. Three times he stops and walks on. The fourth time he opens it.

INT. LABURNAM VILLA - PARLOUR - CONTINUOUS

Mrs. White hides her apron under a cushion and lets him in. He picks a thread from his trousers rather than look at her.

STRANGER
I was asked to call. I come from Maw and Meggins.

MRS. WHITE
Is anything the matter? Has anything happened to Herbert?

STRANGER
Badly hurt. But he is not in any pain.

MRS. WHITE
Oh, thank God! Thank God for that! Thank...

She understands. She takes her husband's hand.

STRANGER
He was caught in the machinery.

MR. WHITE
(dazed)
Caught in the machinery. Yes.

STRANGER
Maw and Meggins disclaim all responsibility. But in consideration of your son's services, they wish to present you with a certain sum as compensation.

MR. WHITE
How much?

STRANGER
Two hundred pounds.

Mrs. White shrieks. Mr. White smiles faintly, puts out his hands like a blind man, and falls.


EXT. NEW CEMETERY - DAY

Two miles out. Two old people at a fresh grave, and nobody else.

INT. LABURNAM VILLA - BEDROOM - NIGHT

A week later. Darkness. Mr. White wakes alone. His wife is weeping at the window.

MR. WHITE
Come back. You will be cold.

MRS. WHITE
It is colder for my son.

Later, she sits bolt upright.

MRS. WHITE (CONT'D)
The paw! The monkey's paw!
(crossing to him)
The other two wishes. We've only had one. Go down and get it, and wish our boy alive again.

MR. WHITE
Good God, you are mad!

MRS. WHITE
Do you think I fear the child I have nursed?

INT. LABURNAM VILLA - PARLOUR - NIGHT

He gropes in the dark for the mantelpiece. His fingers find the paw.

INT. LABURNAM VILLA - BEDROOM - NIGHT

MRS. WHITE
Wish!

MR. WHITE
It is foolish and wicked.

MRS. WHITE
Wish!

MR. WHITE
I wish my son alive again.

Nothing. The candle gutters out. They lie listening to the clock.

INT. LABURNAM VILLA - STAIRS - NIGHT

Mr. White goes down for a candle. The match dies. A knock at the front door, so quiet it is almost not there.

He flees back upstairs. A third knock.

INT. LABURNAM VILLA - BEDROOM - NIGHT

MRS. WHITE
What's that?

MR. WHITE
A rat. It passed me on the stairs.

A loud knock.

MRS. WHITE
It's Herbert! It's Herbert!

He holds her arm. She tears free and runs down.

MR. WHITE
For God's sake don't let it in!

INT. LABURNAM VILLA - HALL - NIGHT

She drags back the chain and the bottom bolt. She can't reach the top one, so she drags a chair to the door.

MRS. WHITE
The bolt! Come down. I can't reach it.

INT. LABURNAM VILLA - BEDROOM - NIGHT

Mr. White on hands and knees, groping across the floor. The knocking is a fusillade now. His hand finds the paw.

He whispers something we don't hear.

The knocking stops.

EXT. LABURNAM VILLA - NIGHT

The door swings open on an empty road. A street lamp flickers. Mrs. White's long wail goes up into the wind.

FADE OUT.
